= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose amplitude coordinates in which a quarter-turn and a diagonal <reflection> act as $r(A,B)=(B,-A)$ and $s(A,B)=(-A,B)$. These generate the natural two-dimensional <group representation> of the <dihedral group> $D_4$. An <equivariant dynamical system> satisfies $F(gv)=gF(v)$. The two independent sign changes make $F_A$ odd in $A$ and even in $B$, and $F_B$ odd in $B$ and even in $A$. The quarter-turn equates the two linear coefficients and the two sets of cubic coefficients. No quadratic monomial is allowed. Thus the <square-symmetric cubic steady-state normal form> is
$$
\boxed{A_T=\mu A-aA^3-bAB^2+O(|(A,B)|^5),\qquad B_T=\mu B-aB^3-bA^2B+O(|(A,B)|^5).}
$$
The <bifurcation parameter> has been scaled so that the critical linear <eigenvalue> is $\mu$. Nondegeneracy at cubic order means $a\ne0$, $a+b\ne0$ and $a-b\ne0$. The <equilibrium> equations factor as $A(\mu-aA^2-bB^2)=B(\mu-aB^2-bA^2)=0$. When both components are nonzero, subtracting the two bracketed expressions gives $(a-b)(A^2-B^2)=0$. Therefore the complete leading list of small steady branches is:

* The zero state, with two linear <eigenvalues> $\mu$.
* Four axial states $(\pm\sqrt{\mu/a},0)$ and $(0,\pm\sqrt{\mu/a})$, when $\mu/a>0$. Their <stability matrix> has <eigenvalues> $-2\mu$ and $\mu(1-b/a)$.
* Four diagonal states $(\pm s,\pm s)$, with independent signs and $s^2=\mu/(a+b)>0$. Their <eigenvalues> are $-2\mu$ and $2\mu(b-a)/(a+b)$.

Each family is one orbit of the <dihedral group>. Each nonzero state retains one <reflection> fixing its amplitude line; its full spatial symmetry depends also on the chosen planforms. Higher-order terms perturb the branch amplitudes but preserve the nondegenerate local classification. For $\mu>0$, an axial branch is <asymptotically stable> precisely when $a>0$ and $b>a$, while a diagonal branch is <asymptotically stable> precisely when $a+b>0$ and $b<a$. Every cubic branch lying on $\mu<0$ has a positive radial <eigenvalue> $-2\mu$, so it is unstable. In the exceptional isotropic cubic case $a=b$, the cubic truncation instead has a circle of <equilibria>; higher-order anisotropy is then needed, which is why that case was excluded.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The third planform is fixed by a diagonal <reflection> and changes sign under a quarter-turn. Extend the preceding <group representation> by
$$
r(A,B,C)=(B,-A,-C),\qquad s(A,B,C)=(-A,B,C).
$$
The other diagonal <reflection> changes only the sign of $B$. Consequently the first two components of an <equivariant dynamical system> have factors $A$ and $B$, whereas its third component is even in each of $A,B$. The quarter-turn exchanges $A^2$ and $B^2$ while negating $C$. Its admissible quadratic coupling is therefore $A^2-B^2$ in the third equation and opposite $AC,BC$ couplings in the first two. At cubic order the only new terms are $AC^2,BC^2,C^3,C(A^2+B^2)$. The linear action on the two-dimensional irreducible mode has one coefficient $\mu_1$, while the distinct scalar representation has its own coefficient $\mu_2$. A small splitting of the two marginal linear modes supplies these two unfolding parameters. With real coefficients the resulting <amplitude equations> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
A_T&=\mu_1A-\alpha_0A^3-\lambda AB^2-\alpha_1AC-\alpha_2AC^2,\\
B_T&=\mu_1B-\alpha_0B^3-\lambda A^2B+\alpha_1BC-\alpha_2BC^2,\\
C_T&=\mu_2C-\gamma_0C^3+\gamma_1(A^2-B^2)-\gamma_2C(A^2+B^2).
\end{aligned}
$$
These equations follow by listing all equivariant monomials through degree three; the displayed minus signs are coefficient conventions, not consequences of symmetry.

In the specified reduced system put $A=B=s$, $C=0$. A nonzero branch exists for $\mu_1>0$, with $q=s^2=\mu_1/(1+\lambda)$. Write perturbations as $u=(\delta A+\delta B)/2$, $v=(\delta A-\delta B)/2$, and $c=\delta C$. <Linearization> gives
$$
u_T=-2\mu_1u,\qquad
\begin{pmatrix}v\\c\end{pmatrix}_T=
\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix}p&-\alpha_1s\\4\gamma_1s&\mu_2\end{pmatrix}}_{M}
\begin{pmatrix}v\\c\end{pmatrix},\qquad p=2(\lambda-1)q>0.
$$
For the <square-pattern interaction with a sign-changing scalar mode>, the remaining <eigenvalues> have sum $\tau=p+\mu_2$ and product $D=p\mu_2+4\alpha_1\gamma_1q=p(\mu_2+K)$, where $K=2\alpha_1\gamma_1/(\lambda-1)>0$. A real two-dimensional <stability matrix> has both <eigenvalues> in the left half-plane exactly when its <trace> is negative and its <determinant> is positive. Therefore
$$
\boxed{-K<\mu_2<-p,\qquad 0<\mu_1<\frac{(1+\lambda)\alpha_1\gamma_1}{(\lambda-1)^2}.}
$$
If $\mu_2<-K$, the block has negative <determinant> and one growing direction, explaining the instability at sufficiently small $\mu_2$. At the lower endpoint $\mu_2=-K$, one <eigenvalue> is zero and the other is $p-K<0$. The symmetry $(A,B,C)\mapsto(B,A,-C)$ fixes the diagonal state and negates its critical $(v,c)$ direction. Thus a generic nonlinear unfolding has a <pitchfork bifurcation> to unequal-amplitude states with nonzero $C$.

At the upper endpoint $\mu_2=-p$, the <trace> vanishes and
$$
\boxed{\omega_H^2=\det M=p(K-p)>0.}
$$
The symmetric <eigenvalue> remains negative. The critical conjugate pair crosses the imaginary axis with <derivative> $d\operatorname{Re}\sigma/d\mu_2=1/2$. If its cubic <Hopf normal form> coefficient is nonzero, this is a <Hopf bifurcation> and nearby periodic solutions exist on one side. Their stability and the side of existence depend on that coefficient; the linear calculation does not determine them. It does establish the generic possibility of oscillating square patterns. The equality $p=K$ is a double-zero degeneracy, outside this nondegenerate <Hopf bifurcation> argument.

= 2
{parent=Paper 60}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Represent the leading real pattern by $A(T)e^{ik_cx}+\overline A(T)e^{-ik_cx}$. Before forcing, continuous <translation symmetry> acts as $A\mapsto e^{i\theta}A$, and spatial <reflection> acts as $A\mapsto\overline A$. Equivariance excludes quadratic terms and permits a linear term and $|A|^2A$ at cubic order. Symmetry under <reflection> makes their coefficients real. A <supercritical bifurcation> has a saturating cubic coefficient; rescaling amplitude and time makes it $-1$. The imperfection has a Fourier component at the same <wavenumber>, so its resonant projection contributes an additive real coefficient. On the spatially uniform <wave envelope> subspace the <amplitude equation> is therefore $A_T=\mu A-|A|^2A+\alpha$. Allowing modulations would additionally produce the spatial terms of the <real Ginzburg-Landau equation>.

For a forcing pattern moving in the positive $x$ direction, its laboratory-frame coefficient is $\alpha e^{-i\Omega T}$, where $\Omega$ is proportional to $k_cc$ after converting to slow time. The comoving amplitude is $B=e^{i\Omega T}A$, since the fast carrier becomes $e^{ik_c(x-ct)}$. Differentiation gives
$$
\boxed{B_T=(\mu+i\Omega)B-|B|^2B+\alpha.}
$$
A constant phase choice makes $\alpha>0$, and <complex conjugation> reverses the sign of $\Omega$, so take $\Lambda=\Omega>0$. Put $B=\alpha^{1/3}a$ and $\tau=\alpha^{2/3}T$. The rescaled linear coefficients are $\mu/\alpha^{2/3}$ and $\Lambda/\alpha^{2/3}$, and the forcing becomes one. Below the rescaled variables are renamed $A,T,\mu,\Lambda$, giving the <directly forced cubic amplitude equation>.

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $s=R^2$. A steady <complex amplitude> obeys $(\mu-s+i\Lambda)A=-1$, so
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{s-\mu+i\Lambda}{(\mu-s)^2+\Lambda^2},\qquad s[(\mu-s)^2+\Lambda^2]=1.}
$$
Thus every positive intensity root gives exactly one steady phase, and $s=0$ is impossible. The cubic intensity <polynomial> is $F(s,\mu)=s^3-2\mu s^2+(\mu^2+\Lambda^2)s-1$. Its positive steady branch can be parameterized completely as
$$
\mu_\pm(s)=s\pm\sqrt{s^{-1}-\Lambda^2},\qquad 0<s\leq\Lambda^{-2},\qquad R=\sqrt s.
$$
The two formulae join smoothly as a single curve at the maximum radius $R=\Lambda^{-1}$; this joining point is not a <saddle-node bifurcation>. A turning point with respect to $\mu$ satisfies $F_s=0$. Subtracting the <equilibrium> and <derivative> conditions gives
$$
\mu-s=\frac1{2s^2},\qquad
\boxed{\Lambda^2=f(s):=\frac1s-\frac1{4s^4},\qquad \mu=s+\frac1{2s^2}.}
$$
These points lie on $\mu_+$. Since $f'(s)=(1-s^3)/s^5$, its maximum is $f(1)=3/4$. Therefore **there is exactly one distinct steady state for every $\mu$ when $\Lambda\geq\sqrt3/2$**. One can also see uniqueness directly: $\mu_-$ is increasing and $\mu_+$ is decreasing when no turning points exist, and their respective ranges meet only at their common endpoint. At the equality there is a <cusp bifurcation> with a triple intensity root at $s=1,\mu=3/2$.

When $0<\Lambda<\sqrt3/2$, the two turning intensities satisfy $s_-<1<s_+$. Define $\mu_L=s_-+1/(2s_-^2)$ and $\mu_U=s_++1/(2s_+^2)$; the curve has a local minimum at the former and a local maximum at the latter, with $\mu_L<\mu_U$. There are three distinct steady intensities for $\mu_L<\mu<\mu_U$, two distinct ones at an endpoint, and one otherwise. The <bifurcation diagrams> below show the folded small-$\Lambda$ curves and the unfurled large-$\Lambda$ curve, together with the <linear stability> calculated next. Both ends of the full branch have $R\to0$ as $|\mu|\to\infty$, while its middle reaches the finite maximum $\Lambda^{-1}$.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let the <equilibrium> be $A_*=Re^{i\theta}$ and write $\delta A=e^{i\theta}(u+iv)$. The <linearization> of the cubic term gives $\delta(|A|^2A)=2s\delta A+A_*^2\delta\overline A$. Therefore the real <stability matrix> is
$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}\mu-3s&-\Lambda\\\Lambda&\mu-s\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its <eigenvalues> obey
$$
\boxed{\sigma^2+2(2s-\mu)\sigma+(3s-\mu)(s-\mu)+\Lambda^2=0,\qquad \sigma_\pm=\mu-2s\pm\sqrt{s^2-\Lambda^2}.}
$$
The square root may be imaginary; stability uses real parts. The <determinant> $D$ is exactly $F_s$, so a zero <eigenvalue> occurs only at the turning points derived above. Away from the cusp those are ordinary <saddle-node bifurcations>; the parameterization joining point has $D>0$.

At a candidate <Hopf bifurcation>, the <trace> vanishes: $\mu_H=2s_H$. Substitution into the steady cubic gives $s_H(s_H^2+\Lambda^2)=1$, which has one positive root. The pair is genuinely imaginary only if
$$
\omega_H^2=D_H=\Lambda^2-s_H^2=\frac1{s_H}-2s_H^2>0.
$$
Thus $s_H<2^{-1/3}$ and, equivalently,
$$
\boxed{\Lambda>2^{-1/3},\qquad \mu_H=2s_H,\qquad \omega_H=\sqrt{\Lambda^2-s_H^2}.}
$$
This direct calculation proves the threshold without needing the additional monotonicity assumption. The equality gives a double-zero <eigenvalue>, not an ordinary <Hopf bifurcation>. Along a fixed-$\Lambda$ branch, implicit differentiation gives $ds/d\mu=-2s(\mu-s)/D$; at this crossing $d\operatorname{Re}\sigma/d\mu=1+4s_H^2/D_H>0$. A nonzero nonlinear Hopf coefficient then gives the usual <Hopf bifurcation>.

The <fold and Hopf thresholds of a directly forced cubic amplitude> divide the diagrams into three regimes. At noncritical points the two <eigenvalues>, counted with multiplicity by the signs of their real parts, are classified as follows:

* For $\Lambda>\sqrt3/2$, there is one <equilibrium> for every $\mu$. It has two stable <eigenvalues> for $\mu<\mu_H$ and two unstable <eigenvalues> for $\mu>\mu_H$.
* For $\sqrt3/2>\Lambda>2^{-1/3}$, the largest-amplitude branch has two stable <eigenvalues>; the intermediate-amplitude branch has one stable and one unstable <eigenvalue>. The smallest-amplitude branch emerging from the lower fold has two stable <eigenvalues> up to its Hopf crossing, and two unstable <eigenvalues> beyond it. The relative location of the Hopf point and the other fold in $\mu$ is not fixed throughout this interval.
* For $0<\Lambda<2^{-1/3}$, the largest-amplitude branch again has two stable <eigenvalues>, the intermediate branch has one of each, and the smallest-amplitude branch has two unstable <eigenvalues>. The trace-zero point lies on the saddle segment and is not a <Hopf bifurcation> because its <determinant> is negative.

At a fold one <eigenvalue> is zero; at a Hopf point the pair has zero real part. These nonhyperbolic points are marked separately in the sketches.

